    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Kruskal's or Prim's?",
      "question": "When do you pick Kruskal's over Prim's, and which properties make greedy MSTs correct?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. When do you pick Kruskal's over Prim's? And which properties make the greedy choice correct? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Kruskal's or Prim's? And why is <em>greedy</em> correct?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Kruskal's is edge-first. Sort every edge and use union-find to reject cycles, which suits sparse graphs. Prim's is vertex-first. Grow one tree with a min-heap of crossing edges, like Dijkstra, which suits dense graphs. Both run in E log E.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Edge-first or <em>vertex-first</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "sort",
              "label": "Kruskal's · sparse graphs",
              "title": "Sort + union-find",
              "code": "sort(edges by weight);\nfor (auto& e : edges)\n  if (dsu.unite(e.u, e.v))\n    mst.push_back(e);",
              "body": "Also yields a forest when disconnected."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "grow",
              "label": "Prim's · dense graphs",
              "title": "Min-heap of crossing edges",
              "code": "auto [w, u, v] = pq.top(); pq.pop();\nif (inMST[v]) continue;  // stale\ninMST[v] = true; total += w;\nfor (auto [x, wx] : adj[v]) pq.push({wx, v, x});",
              "body": "O(V²) array version for complete graphs."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "both",
              "text": "Both are O(E log E).",
              "code": "LC 1584 · LC 1135"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Four facts worth quoting. A spanning tree has exactly V minus one edges. The cut property: the cheapest edge across any cut belongs to some MST. The cycle property: the heaviest edge on a cycle never does. And an MST is not a shortest-path tree. It minimizes the total, not any single route.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Four facts to <em>quote</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "exactly",
                "label": "Size",
                "title": "V − 1 edges",
                "body": "Fewer: disconnected. More: a cycle.",
                "code": "spanning tree"
              },
              {
                "at": "cut",
                "label": "Cut property",
                "title": "Cheapest crossing edge",
                "body": "Always safe to add.",
                "code": "Prim's"
              },
              {
                "at": "heaviest",
                "label": "Cycle property",
                "title": "Heaviest on a cycle",
                "body": "Never needed.",
                "code": "Kruskal's skip"
              },
              {
                "at": "minimizes",
                "label": "Not shortest paths",
                "title": "Total, not routes",
                "body": "Different objective from Dijkstra.",
                "code": "sum of weights"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
